WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
Every winter, people with the flu ask their

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.080
doctor for antibiotics.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.280
The antibiotics do nothing to the flu virus.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.440
But a flu antiviral called oseltamivir can shorten

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.520
the illness by about a day, if it's

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.320
started within two days of the first symptoms.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.720
So why does one drug miss the virus

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.600
completely, and why does the other only help

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:22.880
if you take it early?

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.460
You've seen the rule already.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.580
A useful drug harms the pathogen much more

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.460
than the host.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.940
That's called selective toxicity.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.820
Often the drug hits something the pathogen has

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.220
and your cells don't, or have in a

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:38.100
different form.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.980
A bacterium is a whole cell, so it

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.460
has plenty of parts that differ from ours.

00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:48.186
Penicillin stops a bacterium building its cell wall,

00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:50.666
a tough mesh that stops the cell swelling

00:00:50.747 --> 00:00:51.546
and bursting.

00:00:52.107 --> 00:00:54.586
So when a growing bacterium can't strengthen its

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:57.706
wall, water rushes in by osmosis, and the

00:00:57.787 --> 00:00:58.826
cell bursts.

00:00:59.387 --> 00:01:02.026
Your cells have no wall, so penicillin leaves

00:01:02.107 --> 00:01:03.066
them alone.

00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:07.306
Other antibiotics jam bacterial ribosomes, which are shaped

00:01:07.387 --> 00:01:10.266
differently from ours, so the bacterium can't make

00:01:10.347 --> 00:01:11.226
proteins.

00:01:12.434 --> 00:01:13.993
Now look at a virus.

00:01:14.194 --> 00:01:16.753
It's genes in a protein coat, with no

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:19.073
wall and no ribosomes of its own.

00:01:19.714 --> 00:01:22.833
Inside your cell, it uses your cell's ribosomes

00:01:22.914 --> 00:01:24.273
to make new viruses.

00:01:24.754 --> 00:01:27.313
So an antibiotic has nothing in the virus

00:01:27.394 --> 00:01:27.873
to hit.

00:01:28.434 --> 00:01:30.513
But it still acts on the bacteria living

00:01:30.554 --> 00:01:33.153
in your throat and gut, so antibiotics for

00:01:33.234 --> 00:01:36.433
a cold can help resistant bacteria multiply.

00:01:37.634 --> 00:01:40.513
So how can any drug stop a virus?

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:43.193
Viruses bring a few tools of their own.

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:46.993
Flu carries an enzyme called neuraminidase, which cuts

00:01:47.074 --> 00:01:49.153
new viruses free from the cell that made

00:01:49.274 --> 00:01:49.593
them.

00:01:49.794 --> 00:01:53.233
Oseltamivir blocks that enzyme, so the new viruses

00:01:53.354 --> 00:01:55.633
stay stuck to the cell and can't infect

00:01:55.714 --> 00:01:56.673
more cells.

00:01:56.914 --> 00:02:00.513
Other antivirals block a virus's own copying enzyme,

00:02:00.594 --> 00:02:02.193
or stop it getting into a cell.

00:02:02.594 --> 00:02:06.113
Many antivirals target viral enzymes like these, but

00:02:06.194 --> 00:02:07.873
some block a part of your cell the

00:02:07.954 --> 00:02:10.753
virus needs, such as its entry receptor.

00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:16.286
Antivirals don't destroy virus particles that already exist.

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:19.326
They stop new ones being made or released.

00:02:19.407 --> 00:02:22.819
So with flu, the antiviral helps most early,

00:02:22.954 --> 00:02:24.926
before the virus has spread through millions of

00:02:25.047 --> 00:02:26.393
airway cells.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.486
Most antivirals are also narrow, though some act

00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:32.766
on several virus families, and each disease still

00:02:32.847 --> 00:02:35.086
needs its own clinical evidence.

00:02:35.127 --> 00:02:37.726
And because viruses rely so heavily on our

00:02:37.967 --> 00:02:41.006
cells, safe targets are rare, so there are

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:43.726
far fewer antivirals than antibiotics.

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:47.413
Both kinds of drug can stop working, and

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:48.493
you've seen why.

00:02:48.774 --> 00:02:51.253
A drug stops the microbes it can, and

00:02:51.334 --> 00:02:54.293
the few that already carry resistance survive and

00:02:54.614 --> 00:02:55.573
multiply.

00:02:55.614 --> 00:02:57.653
The drug selects resistance.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:02:59.333
It doesn't create it.

00:02:59.547 --> 00:03:02.293
Bacteria also pass resistance genes to each other

00:03:02.374 --> 00:03:05.893
on plasmids, small loops of DNA, even between

00:03:06.054 --> 00:03:07.493
different species.

00:03:07.534 --> 00:03:10.293
And it's the bacteria that become resistant, never

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:11.093
the person.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.740
HIV copies itself billions of times a day,

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.020
with frequent copying errors, so resistant variants keep

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:19.940
appearing.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.780
Given one drug at a time, HIV in

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.740
many people became resistant.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:29.100
So from 1996, doctors combined three drugs, hitting

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.060
at least two different steps.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:34.140
To survive, one virus would need resistance to

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.500
all three at once, and that's very unlikely.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.660
So resistance spreads fastest where drugs are used

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:42.540
the most.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.580
That's why antibiotics shouldn't be used for viral

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.460
infections, or given routinely to healthy farm animals.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.780
Patients should take them exactly as prescribed, and

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.460
never share leftovers.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.660
And hospitals keep some antibiotics in reserve, for

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.980
infections nothing else can treat.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.600
So how effective are these drugs?

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.680
Judge them on how safely they hit the

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.880
pathogen, how many infections they treat, and how

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.480
long they keep working.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.600
Antibiotics hit parts our cells lack or make

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.280
differently, and oseltamivir fits the flu enzyme far

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.840
better than ours, so both can spare our

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:20.680
cells.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.080
Antibiotics can cure most bacterial infections.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.720
Antivirals are narrower, though for HIV, combinations can

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.880
control an infection for decades.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.280
But every use selects resistant microbes, so careless

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.560
use can make a drug stop working.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.000
Resistance already costs lives.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.400
In 2021, resistant bacteria directly caused about one

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.800
point one million deaths worldwide.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.600
So both work well, but only on the

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.720
right pathogen, and only while they're used carefully.

00:04:53.934 --> 00:04:55.493
Two ideas to keep.

00:04:55.774 --> 00:04:58.773
Antivirals block a step the virus needs.

00:04:59.054 --> 00:05:02.653
And for resistance, the drug selects resistance; it

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:03.773
doesn't create it.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.640
Two exam questions.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:07.640
First.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.240
Virus V copies its RNA using an enzyme

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.000
called V polymerase.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.120
Human cells have no enzyme like it.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.920
Drug K blocks V polymerase.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.200
A patient infected with virus V asks for

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.640
penicillin instead.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.480
Explain why drug K, but not penicillin, can

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.200
treat the infection, and why drug K works

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.440
best soon after symptoms start.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:34.360
Pause here to answer.

00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:47.726
Drug K blocks an enzyme only the virus

00:05:47.807 --> 00:05:50.846
uses, so it stops new viruses being made

00:05:51.007 --> 00:05:52.926
without harming the patient's cells.

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:56.606
Penicillin stops bacteria building a wall, and a

00:05:56.647 --> 00:05:59.566
virus has no wall, so penicillin has nothing

00:05:59.647 --> 00:06:00.846
to act on.

00:06:00.887 --> 00:06:03.326
And drug K works best early, because it

00:06:03.407 --> 00:06:06.526
stops new copies, but it doesn't destroy viruses

00:06:06.607 --> 00:06:08.046
already made.

00:06:08.087 --> 00:06:10.766
Saying the virus is resistant to penicillin is

00:06:10.847 --> 00:06:12.206
the classic mistake.

00:06:13.434 --> 00:06:13.993
Second.

00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:17.593
In an untreated patient, virus W makes about

00:06:17.754 --> 00:06:19.913
ten billion new particles a day.

00:06:20.474 --> 00:06:23.033
About one particle in a hundred thousand is

00:06:23.114 --> 00:06:24.793
resistant to drug A.

00:06:25.114 --> 00:06:26.873
The same is true for drugs B and

00:06:27.034 --> 00:06:30.833
C, and resistance to each drug arises separately.

00:06:31.194 --> 00:06:33.833
Explain why treating with drugs A, B and

00:06:33.994 --> 00:06:36.873
C together is more effective than using drug

00:06:37.034 --> 00:06:38.193
A alone.

00:06:38.554 --> 00:06:39.833
Pause here to answer.

00:06:50.234 --> 00:06:53.193
With drug A alone, about a hundred thousand

00:06:53.274 --> 00:06:56.233
new particles a day are already resistant.

00:06:56.314 --> 00:06:59.673
That's ten billion divided by a hundred thousand.

00:06:59.754 --> 00:07:02.793
They survive and multiply, so drug A soon

00:07:02.874 --> 00:07:03.993
fails.

00:07:04.074 --> 00:07:07.273
To survive all three, one particle needs resistance

00:07:07.354 --> 00:07:10.553
to A, B and C at once, about

00:07:10.634 --> 00:07:12.873
one in a million billion.

00:07:12.954 --> 00:07:15.353
That's fewer than one a century.

00:07:15.394 --> 00:07:17.593
Saying the drugs make the virus mutate is

00:07:17.674 --> 00:07:19.033
the classic mistake.

00:07:20.234 --> 00:07:23.113
So for any drug question, ask why the

00:07:23.194 --> 00:07:26.073
drug harms the pathogen more than our cells,

00:07:26.154 --> 00:07:28.713
which step it blocks, and how resistance could

00:07:28.794 --> 00:07:29.513
be selected.

00:07:30.234 --> 00:07:33.913
Next, a practical: testing what stops bacteria growing

00:07:33.994 --> 00:07:34.793
on agar.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.440
Here are the key notes.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.600
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:40.480
to copy them.
